{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Abraham added and took a wife.</b> This is Hagar; an analogy: 'and YHWH added to speak to me yet again' (דבר אלי עוד). The Torah teaches proper conduct, that a widower who has grown children should not marry a wife until they marry. Therefore it is written 'and he took Rebekah and she became his wife,' and immediately after it: 'and Abraham added and took a wife.'" "<b>And her name was Keturah.</b> Rashi explains: this is Hagar, and she is called Keturah because her deeds were pleasant like incense. And if you ask: earlier, regarding 'and she strayed' (ותתע), Rashi explained that she reverted to the idols of her father's house — one may answer: before she reverted, Abraham took her back a second time, and she repented, and was called Keturah on that account. Another interpretation: she sought to revert to her father's idols but did not revert. And according to the plain sense, this is not Hagar."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:05:45.618624469Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}